{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg9w4Tvk9hShoMqJzGeWX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I changed `Hijack()` in `response_writer.go` so it only refuses to hijack once body bytes have been written (`size > 0`). Before, it also refused after only the headers had been sent (`size == 0`, e.g. via `WriteHeaderNow`). That broke handlers that send headers before hijacking, which is the usual pattern for websocket upgrades.\n\nI added a test case to `TestResponseWriterHijackAfterWrite`: hijacking after `WriteHeaderNow()` should succeed. Hijacking after a body write still returns `errHijackAlreadyWritten`.\n\nGATE PASS: the new test fails without the fix and passes with it.\n\nI only ran the hijack tests, not the full suite. I also didn't try it with a real websocket library; \"send headers, then hijack\" is the pattern I assumed from the commit title."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1732,"cache_read_input_tokens":33455,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1732,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":278,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34eec84d-1de5-448b-b23c-e251de809349","uuid":"075a9a80-49e9-469b-9320-c3740c09e5ff","timestamp":"2026-10-18T15:23:37.569Z","request_id":"req_011Cg9w4TiLafhHMAhhR17nm"}
